DEFINITION: a sequence of NODES, each storing a value and a pointer to the
next node (singly linked) and optionally the previous node (doubly).
   head -> [3 | *] -> [7 | *] -> [9 | None]
OPERATIONS & COST
   insert/delete at the head          O(1)
   insert/delete after a known node   O(1)
   access k-th element / search       O(n)  (must walk)
   insert at the tail                 O(n) singly (O(1) with a tail pointer)
PYTHON SKETCH
   class Node:
       def __init__(self, val, nxt=None):
           self.val = val; self.next = nxt
   def push_front(head, val):   return Node(val, head)    # O(1)
   def find(head, x):
       cur = head
       while cur is not None:
           if cur.val == x: return cur
           cur = cur.next
       return None                                         # O(n)
EDGE CASES: empty list (head is None); one element; deleting the head;
cycles (infinite loop; detect with slow/fast pointers).
MISTAKES: losing the rest of the list by overwriting a pointer before
saving it; not updating head; null-pointer errors.
ARRAY vs LINKED LIST: arrays win at indexing and memory locality;
linked lists win at cheap insertion/deletion when you already hold the
node, but are slower in practice for most workloads.

